.\" beryllium-sandbox(7): what Beryllium confines, by what means, and how
.\" to check it.
.\" Source: man/beryllium-sandbox.7 in the Beryllium repository.
.\" The port replaces @@VERSION@@ and the /usr/local prefix at install.
.\" Every statement here must agree with the feature matrix
.\" (plan/13-FEATURE-MATRIX.md); this page claims nothing a test does not
.\" back.
.\" One sentence per line; check with freebsd/tools/check-man.
.Dd November 16, 2026
.Dt BERYLLIUM-SANDBOX 7
.Os Beryllium @@VERSION@@
.Sh NAME
.Nm beryllium-sandbox
.Nd process confinement in the Beryllium web browser
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr beryllium 1
runs as several processes.
The browser process owns the windows, the profile and the network
identity of the user.
A renderer runs the content of one site.
A GPU process draws, and utility processes handle the network, storage
and audio.
A sandbox confines the processes that handle untrusted data, so that a
fault in one of them does not give a web page the authority of the user's
account.
.Pp
This page states what Beryllium enforces, by what means, and how to check
it from outside the browser.
It describes the release it is installed with.
.Ss Status
By default no process is confined.
A compromised web page is not contained, as with the Chromium port for
.Fx .
.Bl -column "Network, storage, audio" "Status" -offset indent
.It Sy Process Ta Sy Status
.It Browser Ta not confined
.It Renderers Ta not confined by default; lockdown below
.It GPU Ta not confined
.It Network, storage, audio Ta not confined
.El
.Pp
The page
.Li beryllium://gpu
reports
.Dq Sandboxed: false
for the GPU process, which is the kernel's answer and not a claim.
.Ss Measures in every process
Four measures apply to every child process, whether or not the lockdown
is on.
They limit what a compromised process can reach.
They do not confine it.
.Bl -bullet
.It
The descriptors the browser has open are closed between
.Xr fork 2
and
.Xr execve 2 ,
so a child starts with the descriptors meant for it and no others.
.It
Shared memory is anonymous, made with
.Xr memfd_create 2 :
it has no name in any file system, its size is sealed, and a region
shared read-only is read-only by its Capsicum rights.
.It
A renderer is given no swap file, so page content parked by a background
tab stays in memory.
.It
From 0.20.3.22 a child process gives up the means to gain privileges
.Pq Dv PROC_NO_NEW_PRIVS_CTL , see Xr procctl 2 :
if it executes a set-user-ID file, nothing is gained.
The browser process does not, because it starts helper programs.
.Cm BerylliumChildNoNewPrivileges
switches this off;
.Ql proccontrol -m nonewprivs -q -p PID
shows it.
.El
.Ss The renderer lockdown
With the feature
.Cm BerylliumCapsicumRenderer
enabled, each renderer does the following before it handles page content:
.Bl -enum
.It
It reads what capability mode would refuse later and keeps the answers:
time zone data, message catalogues, the amount of memory.
.It
It judges every descriptor it holds by the admission rule below and cuts
each one it keeps to the rights of its kind.
.It
It limits its standard streams.
.It
From 0.20.3.22 it gives up what capability mode leaves it and it has no
use for: new privileges, core files, new processes
.Pq Dv RLIMIT_NPROC ,
and being traced
.Pq Dv PROC_TRACE_CTL .
The last means that no other process of the same user can attach a
debugger to it, record its system calls or read its descriptor table.
.It
It calls
.Xr cap_enter 2
and checks the result with
.Xr cap_getmode 2 .
.El
.Pp
A process in capability mode cannot open a file by path, cannot create a
network connection and cannot signal another process; see
.Xr capsicum 4 .
What it can still do is decided by the descriptors it holds and their
rights, which is why the policy below matters as much as the mode.
.Pp
If any step fails, the renderer ends.
A renderer asked to confine itself never runs unconfined.
.Pp
The browser does not take the renderer's word for it.
From 0.20.3.22 it sends a renderer nothing, not even the invitation to
connect, until the kernel says that the process is in capability mode.
A renderer needs nothing from the browser to get that far.
One that has exited, that the kernel gives no answer for, or that is not
there after ten seconds is ended without having been sent anything.
This holds whatever the renderer was told: with
.Fl -no-sandbox ,
under which a renderer skips its lockdown, every renderer is ended at
once and no page loads.
When the renderer has connected the browser asks the kernel once more,
as 0.20.3.21 did, and ends the renderer unless the answer is yes.
No answer counts as no.
.Pp
When it starts, the browser also finds out what the kernel does for a
confined process, by trying it in a child process: whether capability
mode exists, and whether a process in it is refused
.Xr kill 2
and
.Xr sigqueue 2
to another process, which FreeBSD-SA-26:28.capsicum repaired.
A kernel that fails either is named in the log and on the status page.
On a kernel without capability mode every renderer ends at its lockdown.
.Pp
The page
.Pa beryllium://sandbox
lists the browser, every renderer and every other child process with its
capability mode as the kernel gives it when the page is loaded, whether
the browser asked it to lock down, and whether the check passed.
Nothing on it is a process's own report.
A renderer that was launched a moment ago and has not connected yet has
not been checked.
The page lists it as
.Dq not yet (starting)
and counts it apart from the renderers in capability mode; its own
renderer is one whenever the page opens in a new tab.
Reload the page to see what became of it.
A renderer listed as asked to lock down and not attested, without that
word, is one the browser refused and is ending.
The page also gives the result of the kernel check and, for each kind of
process that is not confined, the reason.
.Pp
Other processes that hold a direct link to a renderer, namely the GPU
process, the network service and the storage service, cut every descriptor
they send over it to the rights of its kind, as the browser does.
The browser marks such a link when it makes it.
.Ss Descriptor policy
At the lockdown point a descriptor is admitted by what it is and where it
comes from:
.Bl -bullet
.It
A read-only file is admitted only under
.Pa /usr/local/share/beryllium/ ,
the browser's own resources.
.It
A socket is admitted only as an end of an unnamed
.Dv AF_UNIX
socket pair, stream or sequenced packet, bound to no path and connected
to no path.
.It
A directory is closed.
.It
Anything else, such as a writable file, a device or a terminal, ends the
renderer.
.El
.Pp
Each kind keeps these rights and no others; see
.Xr rights 4 :
.Bl -tag -width "shared memory"
.It read-only file
.Dv CAP_PREAD ,
.Dv CAP_MMAP_R ,
.Dv CAP_FSTAT ,
and
.Dv CAP_FCNTL
for
.Dv F_GETFL .
.It socket
.Dv CAP_RECV ,
.Dv CAP_SEND ,
.Dv CAP_EVENT ,
.Dv CAP_FSTAT ,
.Dv CAP_SHUTDOWN ,
.Dv CAP_GETSOCKOPT ,
.Dv CAP_SETSOCKOPT ,
and
.Dv CAP_FCNTL
for
.Dv F_GETFL
and
.Dv F_SETFL .
Never the rights to connect, bind, listen or accept.
.It kqueue
.Dv CAP_KQUEUE ,
.Dv CAP_EVENT
and
.Dv CAP_FSTAT .
.It eventfd, pipe
.Dv CAP_READ ,
.Dv CAP_WRITE ,
.Dv CAP_EVENT ,
.Dv CAP_FSTAT ,
.Dv CAP_FCNTL
for
.Dv F_GETFL
and
.Dv F_SETFL ,
and
.Dv CAP_IOCTL
for
.Dv FIONREAD
alone.
.It shared memory
.Dv CAP_READ ,
.Dv CAP_WRITE ,
.Dv CAP_SEEK ,
.Dv CAP_MMAP_RW ,
.Dv CAP_FSTAT ,
and
.Dv CAP_FCNTL
for
.Dv F_GETFL ,
less whatever its creator withheld.
.It writable file
Only one the browser hands over on purpose after the lockdown, such as a
file a site was granted through the File System Access interface:
.Dv CAP_PREAD ,
.Dv CAP_PWRITE ,
.Dv CAP_FTRUNCATE ,
.Dv CAP_FSYNC ,
.Dv CAP_MMAP_RW ,
.Dv CAP_FSTAT ,
and
.Dv CAP_FCNTL
for
.Dv F_GETFL .
.El
.Pp
Rights are only ever reduced, with
.Xr cap_rights_limit 2 ,
.Xr cap_fcntls_limit 2
and
.Xr cap_ioctls_limit 2 .
.Pp
Capsicum gives a new descriptor every right, so the policy is applied
wherever a descriptor appears after the lockdown as well:
.Bl -bullet
.It
What a confined renderer creates is cut at creation: the kqueue and
eventfd of each message loop, shared memory, and the socket pairs Mojo
makes.
.It
What it receives over a Mojo channel is cut on arrival.
A received descriptor the policy has no kind for keeps no right at all.
.It
The browser cuts every descriptor it sends a confined renderer before it
sends it, so the limits do not depend on the renderer's cooperation.
.El
.Ss Utility services
Some utility services take bytes from the browser and give bytes back.
From 0.20.3.22 such a service can be locked down as a renderer is, as an
experiment that is off by default.
With
.Cm BerylliumCapsicumUtilities
enabled, the browser tells three services to lock down
.Pq the data decoder, the unzipper and the file patcher ,
cuts what it sends them, and sends them nothing until the kernel says
capability mode.
.Fl -beryllium-confine-services Ns = Ns Ar name , Ns Ar name
names further services to try, by the names
.Fl -utility-sub-type
carries.
None of the three has been measured after its start, so one of them may
end at its lockdown; the log says why.
.Ss Processes that are not confined
Decided on 12 November 2026, following what Chromium on Linux, OpenBSD's
port and Firefox do.
.Bl -tag -width "Storage service"
.It Browser
It holds the profile, the network and the windows of every tab.
Other browsers leave this process unconfined too, except OpenBSD's port,
whose limits still allow files, processes and the network.
.It GPU
The graphics driver opens devices and files by path whenever it likes,
and NVIDIA's is closed code.
.It Network service
It makes every connection and keeps the cache by path.
Confining it needs a broker for both, which is described in the plan and
not scheduled.
.It Storage service
It opens the profile's databases by path.
Chromium has a mode in which the browser opens them for it, which has not
been tried here.
.It Audio service
It reaches the sound server by path; a policy is to follow the utilities.
.It Video capture
It opens cameras by path; Chromium leaves it unconfined on every desktop
system.
.It Other utilities
A utility is confined once a survey shows that it needs nothing by path
after its start.
.El
.Ss What is not covered
The lockdown is experimental, off by default, and not yet a complete
sandbox.
.Bl -bullet
.It
The processes listed above are not confined.
A page that takes over a renderer can still ask them for what they do
for a renderer.
.It
The lockdown has not been run on a Mesa graphics driver, nor on
.Fx 14
or 15.
.It
The switches that undo the lockdown work for any user.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check that every renderer is in capability mode, shown by a
.Sq C
in the flags column:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
procstat -s $(pgrep -f -- --type=renderer)
.Ed
.Pp
List the descriptors of one renderer with their Capsicum rights.
From 0.20.3.22 a locked-down renderer refuses this unless it was started
traceable, with
.Cm BerylliumRendererTraceable
enabled beside the lockdown:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
procstat -fC 12345
.Ed
.Pp
Both need
.Va security.bsd.unprivileged_proc_debug
set to 1.
The page
.Pa beryllium://sandbox
shows capability mode for every process without it.
.Pp
From a checkout of the repository, run the sandbox tests with the lockdown
on.
They then act as a gate: they fail if any renderer is not in capability
mode by the kernel's answer, if its descriptors cannot be read, or if any
descriptor is outside the policy.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
freebsd/tests/browser/run sandbox --lockdown
.Ed
.Pp
Record what capability mode refuses in each process, with
.Xr ktrace 1 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
freebsd/tests/browser/run capsicum --diagnose --lockdown
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
The lockdown writes to the browser's log, which
.Fl -enable-logging Ns = Ns Cm stderr
sends to standard error.
.Bl -diag
.It "beryllium-sandbox: renderer pid=N capability_mode=1 closed_directories=N limited=N threads=N thread_names=... hardening=..."
A renderer locked down.
From 0.20.3.22 the line ends with what else the renderer gave up:
.Li no-new-privileges ,
.Li no-core ,
.Li no-fork ,
and
.Li untraceable
or
.Li traceable .
A utility service that locked down writes the same line with
.Dq utility pid=N service=NAME .
The line counts the directories it closed and the descriptors it limited,
and gives the number and the names of the threads the process had at that
point; there should be one,
.Li CrRendererMain .
Before version 0.20.3.17 there were two, and every name was the process's
own, because the kernel was not told the threads' names.
.It "beryllium-sandbox: renderer pid=N could not enter capability mode: ..."
The lockdown failed and the renderer ended.
The text after the colon names the descriptor that broke the policy, or
the call that failed.
.It "beryllium-sandbox: received ... (fd N), which a confined process may not hold: every right removed"
A confined renderer received a descriptor the policy has no kind for.
The descriptor stays open with no rights.
.It "beryllium-sandbox: socket pair made at ... on thread '...' (fd N, fd N): limited"
A confined renderer made a socket pair; both ends were cut to a socket's
rights.
.It "beryllium-sandbox: cut ... (fd N) bound for confined renderer pid N"
The browser cut a descriptor before sending it.
Logged only with
.Fl -vmodule Ns = Ns Cm capsicum_rights=1 .
.It "beryllium-sandbox: ... bound for confined renderer pid N, which it may not hold: every right removed before sending"
The browser was about to send a confined renderer a descriptor the policy
has no kind for.
.It "beryllium-sandbox: renderer pid=N invited M ms after its launch, in capability mode by the kernel's account"
The browser sent the renderer its invitation, having waited for the
kernel's answer.
.It "beryllium-sandbox: renderer pid=N is not in capability mode by the kernel's account; ended before it was sent anything, M ms after its launch"
The renderer did not lock down, and the browser ended it uninvited.
.It "beryllium-sandbox: renderer pid=N refused in capability mode: syscall=S errno=E thread=T frames: ..."
With
.Cm BerylliumCapsicumTrap
enabled: capability mode refused the renderer a system call.
The frames are where the call was made and the return addresses above
it, each as a module and an offset.
The renderer goes on; before 0.20.3.22 the switch ended it.
.It "beryllium-sandbox: this kernel has no capability mode ..."
.It "beryllium-sandbox: on this kernel a process in capability mode can signal another process ..."
The browser's check of the kernel at start failed.
.El
